[{:seon.task/name "a-processs-heap-ceiling-fits-the-target-machine", :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-14 (deps): the standing root's heap ceiling is 32 GB (jolt-heap-ceiling-bytes 34,359,738,368), twice Seon's 16 GB target machine (AGENTS section 8), because root runs on Jolt's default; its live heap read 1.42 GB then, so the ceiling bounds nothing it should. Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: seon.lib/heap-cap! (src/seon/lib.clj:182) is called only for an agent at its start (src/seon/agent.clj:2184: its heap at the fork plus :seon.config.agent/heap-extra-bytes) and for a test child (agent.clj:1303, the same rule); root's start (seon.system/start!, src/seon/system.clj:1732-1844: open files, sweepers, heap-free ratio) and the fork server never call it; no config key states a ceiling for the target machine. Design: the one start, seon.lib/start-process! (task seon.lib/a-process-the-binary-starts-runs-as-production), sets the ceiling from one documented key, :seon.config.process/heap-ceiling-bytes (schema entry with its reason: sized to the 16 GB target, less the OS's share), read through the one resolver: root and the fork server take it whole; an agent and a test child keep their relative ceiling (heap at fork plus heap-extra-bytes) capped at it; reaching it is the typed OutOfMemoryError it already is. Deletes: nothing (the agent's own call stays, capped). Estimate: one scheme eval at a start (~100k ticks [H]); no per-call cost. Done: each process's jolt-heap-ceiling-bytes is at most that key's value. Proof, red first: seon.lib-test/every-process-starts-under-the-target-machines-heap-ceiling (root, its fork server, an agent and a test child each read their ceiling; red today: root's is 32 GB). docs/machine.md#what-a-process-owns-in-memory.", :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/every-process-starts-under-the-target-machines-heap-ceiling}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-the-binary-starts-runs-as-production"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by deps via tests: a test shard runs Datahike's per-restore log/trace through trove's default *log-fn*, which production turns off, so tests measure a path production does not run. Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: a process's start is written in four partial copies. The binary: gc-sweepers! 0 and end-with! the parent in seon.main/-main (src/seon/main.clj:168-169). Root, after -main: ignore-sigpipe! and trove/set-log-fn! nil unless :log? (src/seon/system.clj:1809-1810), raise-open-files! (:1732), sweepers from config (:1841), max-heap-free-ratio! (:1844). A test shard: gc-sweepers! 0 only (test/test_runner.clj:965). One test namespace: a hand patch trove/set-log-fn! nil at load (test/seon/procs_test.clj:15). Forked processes (agents, test children, clones, the fork server's standby) inherit the parent's log fn, SIGPIPE disposition and sweeper pool, each adding only its role's steps (heap-cap!, intr/install!, intr/forked!, pdb/after-fork!). The dead :noise flood is still there (src/seon/agent_proc.clj:213-217, :311, :317; system.clj:882). Design: one fn, seon.lib/start-process!, the one start (principles §5: what a process runs on is set at the one start), called first by every process started from the binary: (1) gc-sweepers! 0; (2) end-with! the parent when it is not launchd; (3) ignore-sigpipe!; (4) Datahike/trove logging set as production sets it (off); (5) the uncaught handler pointed at the process's own sink; (6) the heap ceiling (seon.lib/a-processs-heap-ceiling-fits-the-target-machine). Callers: seon.main/-main (every binary mode) and test_runner/-main. Root's start! keeps only open files, sweepers from config, heap-free ratio, and :log? turning logging on for checks that ask. The order is the physics (sweepers before anything collects), stated once in the fn. Deletes: test_runner/-main's sweeper call, system/start!'s sigpipe and log-fn lines, procs_test's log-fn patch, the :noise flood. Reach: bin/system start, bin/update, bin/test, bin/prove, bin/check, the standing system, worktree systems, test children by inheritance; shared machinery, so one real bin/prove end to end before it lands. Estimate: two reads of process state, ~10k ticks; no per-call cost. Lands alone, first. Proofs, red first: seon.lib-test/a-process-the-binary-starts-runs-as-production (in a shard, trove's *log-fn* is nil and SIGPIPE is ignored; red today); seon.system-test/the-sweeper-pool-is-fixed-before-anything-collects; the design guard seon.main-test/a-process-is-set-up-only-by-its-one-start (from the compiler's facts: no src form and no test_runner form but seon.lib/start-process! names trove/set-log-fn!, seon.lib/ignore-sigpipe! or seon.lib/gc-sweepers! with 0; red today: seon.main/-main, seon.system/start!, test-runner/-main)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-process-the-binary-starts-runs-as-production seon.main-test/a-process-is-set-up-only-by-its-one-start seon.system-test/the-sweeper-pool-is-fixed-before-anything-collects}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-forks-wait-ends-at-its-declared-bound-naming-its-holders"
  :seon.task/plan "Approved by root 2026-11-14 (deps). Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: seon.lib/fork! (src/seon/lib.clj:257) calls jolt.host/fork-alone (fork-alone*, lib.clj:250), which waits in Chez's S_fork_alone until every other thread parks; only jolt.host/fork-alone-cancel! from another thread ends the wait, and no production caller sets one; its callers: an agent's clone (src/seon/agent.clj:2076), the fork server (src/seon/fork_server.clj:47 and :402); an agent's test children fork through jolt.async.remote.flow/fork! (agent.clj:1353), not fork!, so a bound set in fork! would not reach them. No :seon.config.*/fork-wait-ms key exists (git grep). seon.lib-test/a-fork-whose-wait-is-cancelled-says-how-many-run (test/seon/lib_test.clj:19) spins a canceller. A thread in a plain blocking foreign call never parks, so such a fork waits for good (a test child sat 20 min in S_fork_alone until bin/stalls ended it, runs/evidence/gate-stall-1346; its lost wakeup is fixed in Chez 3ec3794d, the unbounded wait remains). Why the bound is not a Flow message: the forking thread is the one active thread, parked in C waiting for the others; no channel can reach it, so the bound lives in the wait itself, a physics edge (docs/failure-points.md, the fork row). Design: (1) Chez S_fork_alone takes a deadline and waits with s_thread_cond_timedwait, answering -3 at it, the wake still each park (no polling); (2) jolt.host/fork-alone takes the bound in ms and, at -3, answers the holders: each active thread's id, Jolt name and whether it is in C (Chez's thread list, the tc's active and native-state fields); (3) the bound is :seon.config.process/fork-wait-ms, documented with its reason, read through the one resolver; fork!'s refused fn takes {:active-threads n :holders [...] :bound-ms ms}, and the existing typed errors (:seon.test/fork-refused, :seon.fork-server/standby-failed, the clone's :clone-failed) carry :holders; (4) test children route through fork! (seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer (5)), so every fork meets the bound. Overlaps seon.agent/fork-error-names-the-threads (holders named): one of the two goes when this lands. Deletes: fork-alone-cancel! as a bound, the test's spinning canceller. Reach: every fork (agents, test children, clones, the fork server, bin/test shards); shared machinery and a dependency change (Chez, Jolt forks on jolt/<topic> branches), so one real bin/prove end to end. Estimate: a fork's wait beside parked threads is microseconds (6.4 ms max measured, fork(2) included); the bound is the max over a gate run x 10, measured, not guessed. Proofs, red first: seon.lib-test/a-forks-wait-ends-at-its-bound-naming-its-holders (a thread in a plain blocking read holds the fork; fork! answers refused within the bound, its holders naming that thread as in C); the design guard seon.main-test/every-fork-is-lib-fork (red today: fork-test-child's rflow/fork!). AMENDED 2026-11-15 (flow and runtime; the owner's ruling: no clock decides our own work): the bound stays only until its cause is gone. The goal is no clock: fork-alone waits for parks; a waiting fork answers ping with its holders and whether each is in a foreign call (fork-holders plus a Jolt primitive, deps); the class is fixed at its owner, every blocking foreign call collect-safe (deps), and then :seon.config.agent/fork-wait-ms and its refusal go. Not a refusal on 'in C': a short non-collect-safe call (dlsym, stat) parks a moment later (runtime). The order: deps' collect-safe audit first, so removing the bound never brings back the 20-minute wait. [H, not measured]: the 1 s bound may refuse a fork falsely at load 44 (context's 13:32 land lost two shared roots at their first spawn; their logs are kept from 5ea1bbd49e on and will say). Proof then: seon.lib-test/a-fork-beside-a-slow-turn-waits-for-it-and-is-never-refused. flow first filed this as a second task without finding this one, its prior work: merged here."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-fork-beside-a-slow-turn-waits-for-it-and-is-never-refused seon.lib-test/a-forks-wait-ends-at-its-bound-naming-its-holders seon.main-test/every-fork-is-lib-fork}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-reports-the-most-processes-it-held-at-once"
  :seon.task/plan "For the effects owner's admission (seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer (2)): a run's class is weighed by the most processes it held at once, read with no ps (seon.prove/held-processes, src/seon/prove.clj:118, one ps read of every process's environment, ~70 ms a process each grant pass, goes with it). Builder runtime; reader tests (admission). Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521. Done at HEAD, slice (a)'s writer [M]: root's api proc folds its peak as 2 plus its live agents (::peak, src/seon/system.clj:812), delivered at the fork server's end (:901), answered by seon.system/stop! (:2397-2422) and printed on ROOT STOPPED (src/seon/main.clj:68); asserted in seon.system-test/a-stop-writes-the-roots-fibers-before-it-waits. Not done [M]: its reader: seon.test-store/stop-root! (test/seon/test_store.clj:507) does not read the peak; seon.lib/sh and fork! count nothing; the test child's :done carries no peak. What exists of starting [M]: seon.lib/fork! (agents, clones, the fork server), jolt.async.remote.flow/fork! (test children, agent.clj:1353), seon.lib/sh (external programs, synchronous), and bin/held (roots a test starts, detached: re-parented to launchd, and leading their own groups, so neither a tree walk nor a pgid count finds them). Design: each Seon process counts what it starts in the one spawn path, live (+1 at the start, -1 at its exit, the exit already an event: jolt.io-poller/exit-chan), keeping its peak as 1 plus the most of its children's sum at once, each child counted at the peak it reports in the end message its starter already reads: an agent's :seon.process/end, a root's ROOT STOPPED line, a test child's :done (as :seon.fx/peak-processes); an external program counts 1. An upper bound (children's peaks summed though they may not coincide), safe for admission. A test process's shared roots (seon.test-store/shared-root!) outlive the test that started them: a shared root's peak weighs its shard's admission, once. Slices: (a) stop-root! reads root's peak; (b) seon.lib/sh and fork! count a process's own children, and the test child's peak is 1 + its sh children at once + the peaks of the roots it stopped, riding :done; (c) a shared root's peak weighs its shard, from its stop at the shard's end. Estimate: one counter update per spawn and exit (~1k ticks), the proof ~2M ticks. Proof, red first: seon.lib-test/a-process-reports-the-most-processes-it-held-at-once (a child that starts two processes at once, then one, reports 3; a run with no children reports 1). The count is only right if every start passes the counted path: guarded by seon.main-test/every-process-start-is-the-process-kinds (seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.lib-test/a-process-reports-the-most-processes-it-held-at-once seon.main-test/every-process-start-is-the-process-kinds}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}]
